---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { oracleWaitlist } from "../data/elsewhere";

const experiment = [
  { step: "Collect the corrections you keep giving.", detail: "Archive keeps every session with your agent, and Foundry records each correction with what the agent was doing when you gave it." },
  { step: "Give them to your agent before it plans.", detail: "Each correction becomes a change to your docs, or to what one Warden knows, so your agent is reminded of it on the turn it matters." },
  { step: "Measure whether the same correction comes back.", detail: "If you stop having to give it, the change held. If it comes back, the change did not stick, and Oracle tries again." },
];
const youGet = [
  "Oracle runs that experiment on your project, against your own sessions and your own merged work.",
  "It keeps the doc and Warden changes that hold, and drops the ones that don't.",
  "It shows you the numbers: which corrections stopped coming back, and which you still give.",
];
---
<Base
  title="Oracle: your agents stop making the same mistake · inixiative"
  description="Oracle collects the corrections you keep giving your coding agent, gives them to the agent before it plans, and measures whether the same correction comes back. Join the waitlist."
  domain="agentic"
  current="oracle"
>
  <section class="hero">
    <span class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot" data-live></span>Oracle</span>
    <h1 class="display-1">Your agents stop making the same mistake.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Oracle collects the corrections you keep giving your coding agent, gives them to the agent before it
      plans, and measures whether the same correction comes back.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href={oracleWaitlist}>Join the Oracle waitlist</a>
      <a class="button button-secondary" href="/foundry/">Use Foundry today</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>The experiment</span>
      <h2>Testing whether your corrections change how your agent behaves for good.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Models get better, but they keep doing things their own way in your codebase. Oracle tests whether the
        corrections you already give, collected and handed to the agent in advance, change that without
        retraining the model.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ol class="steps">
      {experiment.map((s) => <li><strong>{s.step}</strong> {s.detail}</li>)}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="text">
        <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>What you get</span>
        <h2 class="display-3">The changes that hold, and the numbers that show it.</h2>
        <ul>{youGet.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul>
        <p>
          Each change is tested on work that already shipped: Oracle winds the repo back to before a merged
          change, gives the agent the original ticket, and uses the merged diff as the answer key.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="text">
        <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>Where it stands</span>
        <h2 class="display-3">Running on our own projects now.</h2>
        <p>
          On template's tuning set of 116 real tasks, taken from merged work, the Wardens recognised 81% of
          the moments that touched something the codebase already had (92 of 114), and spoke up wrongly on 2.7%
          of the rest (37 of 1,349).
        </p>
        <p>
          These numbers are early: they were measured on 18 October 2026, on the same set the Wardens were
          tuned on. There is no number yet for how much less often a correction comes back; that is what the
          experiment measures. Oracle is closed source.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="card feature" data-raised>
      <div class="text">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot"></span>Foundry</span>
        <h2 class="display-3">How Oracle's changes reach your agent today.</h2>
        <p>
          Foundry is the workspace around Claude Code and Codex, free to use and source-available. Its Wardens
          remind your agent of what your project already has and where it's written down, on every turn. When
          Oracle changes a doc or a Warden, Foundry is what carries that change into your next session.
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button button-secondary" href="/foundry/">How Foundry works</a>
          <a class="text-link" href="/foundry/demo/">Try the demo</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="waitlist">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow" data-quiet>Waitlist</span>
      <h2>Run Oracle on your project.</h2>
      <p class="lede">Join the waitlist to hear when Oracle opens to other projects, with the results as they come in.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button" href={oracleWaitlist}>Join the Oracle waitlist</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
